  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nA closed upper level low will slowly move eastwards across the\nnorthern portions of MT today. Scattered rain\nshowers/thunderstorms will affect mostly the eastern portions of\nthe CWA this morning, with rain/snow falling over the Rocky\nMountain Front. Snow levels this morning will be around 6500 feet.\nAn inch or two of snow is still possible this morning at ridge\ntops along the divide. Windy conditions develop this afternoon\nover North Central MT, as the upper level low exits the area.\n\nFor Tue through Wednesday, expect drier and not as windy\nconditions over the CWA. Seasonable temperatures are expected.\n\nOn Thursday, the next upper level approaches Southwest MT by late\nin the day. Expect increasing chances for showers/thunderstorms\nover Southwest MT, with the precipitation spreading northward into\nCentral MT by Friday morning. At this time, since this system is\nexpected to be quick moving, precipitation amounts will be light\nside for most areas.\n\nShowers should exit the eastern portions of the CWA on Saturday\nmorning, with generally dry conditions the rest of Saturday and\ninto early next week. Afternoon temperatures will likely be near\nnormal next weekend as well.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe main concern today will be snow levels over the Rockies this\nmorning and windy conditions over the Plains by this afternoon.\n\nOverall, temperatures have cooled to near freezing along the\ndivide for elevations above 6500 feet this morning. Light precip\nshould redevelop this morning as the upper level low moves\nthrough. Day time heating will limit snow accumulations, thus no\nwinter weather advisory for snow will be issued at this time.\n\nIn terms of winds, it is still looking like Fergus County will\nhave the highest potential for strong wind gusts over 55 mph\ntoday, so the high wind watch was changed to a high wind warning\nfor that county. For Judith Basin, the overall probability of\nwarning wind gusts is generally less than 60 percent, thus the\nhigh wind watch was cancelled. Brusda",
